/**
\defgroup gamma Gamma function and polygamma
\brief Logarithm of the gamma function, \f$\log \Gamma(x)\f$, digamma, trigamma, and polygamma functions to order 6.

This module evaluates \f$\log \Gamma(x)\f$ along with digamma, trigamma, and polygamma functions up to order 6. Include `<laplace/math/special.hpp>` for the primary `laplace::math` functions, or `<laplace/math/polygamma.hpp>` (included by `special.hpp`) for Laplace's own `laplace::math::own` routines. All functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` or `LAPLACE_HD_FORCEINLINE` and run on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device. In tests, `lgamma()`, `digamma()`, and `trigamma()` stay within 2.42 ulp on gcc and CUDA, while `polygamma()` for orders 2 to 6 stays within 3.36 to 9.44 ulp.

## Mathematical definitions

The module defines the log-gamma function and polygamma derivatives on the positive real line. For \f$x \in (0, \infty)\f$, the logarithm of the gamma function is
\f[
\operatorname{lgamma}(x) = \log \Gamma(x).
\f]
For integer order \f$n \in \{0, \dots, 6\}\f$ and argument \f$x \in (0, \infty)\f$, the polygamma function (DLMF §5.2) is
\f[
\psi^{(n)}(x) = \frac{\mathrm{d}^n}{\mathrm{d}x^n} \psi(x) = \frac{\mathrm{d}^{n+1}}{\mathrm{d}x^{n+1}} \log \Gamma(x).
\f]
The case \f$n = 0\f$ is the digamma function \f$\psi(x) = \Gamma'(x)/\Gamma(x)\f$, and \f$n = 1\f$ is the trigamma function \f$\psi'(x) = \psi_1(x)\f$.

## Domain and special values

All functions require \f$x > 0\f$, and `polygamma()` supports integer orders \f$n \in \{0, \dots, 6\}\f$. Log-gamma vanishes at \f$x = 1.0\f$ and \f$x = 2.0\f$. Digamma is strictly increasing on \f$(0, \infty)\f$ and vanishes at \f$x_0 \approx 1.4616321449683623\f$, while trigamma is strictly positive and decreasing.

For \f$n < 0\f$, \f$n > 6\f$, or NaN inputs, \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma()" and \ref laplace::math::own::polygamma "own::polygamma()" return quiet NaN. When \f$x \le 0\f$, `own::polygamma()` (and `polygamma()` for \f$n \ge 2\f$) returns quiet NaN, while `lgamma()`, `digamma()`, `trigamma()`, and `polygamma()` for \f$n \in \{0, 1\}\f$ call Boost.Math directly. As \f$x \to 0^+\f$, \f$\log \Gamma(x) \to +\infty\f$, \f$\psi(x) \to -\infty\f$, \f$\psi'(x) \to +\infty\f$, and \f$\psi^{(n)}(x) \sim (-1)^{n+1} n!\, x^{-(n+1)}\f$ overflows to \f$\pm\infty\f$.

## Numerical methods

The wrappers in `<laplace/math/special.hpp>` combine Boost.Math for orders up to 1 with Laplace's own routine for higher orders. Specifically, \ref laplace::math::lgamma "lgamma()", \ref laplace::math::digamma "digamma()", and \ref laplace::math::trigamma "trigamma()" forward to Boost.Math under a policy that disables exceptions and `double` to `long double` promotion (Boost.Math), compiling on host and CUDA (`BOOST_MATH_GPU_ENABLED`). The dispatch function \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma()" forwards \f$n = 0\f$ to `digamma()`, \f$n = 1\f$ to `trigamma()`, and \f$n \in \{2, \dots, 6\}\f$ to \ref laplace::math::own::polygamma "own::polygamma()", as Boost.Math provides no GPU implementation for \f$n \ge 2\f$.

The routine `own::polygamma()` evaluates in two stages:

1. For \f$x < 15.0\f$, the argument shifts to \f$z = x + m \ge 15.0\f$ with integer shift count \f$m = \lfloor 15.0 - x \rfloor + 1\f$ (and \f$m = 0\f$ for \f$x \ge 15.0\f$) via
   \f[
   \psi^{(n)}(x) = \psi^{(n)}(x + m) + (-1)^{n+1} n! \sum_{j=0}^{m-1} (x + j)^{-(n+1)},
   \f]
   where \f$j \in \{0, \dots, m-1\}\f$ is the summation index.
2. At \f$z \ge 15.0\f$, the asymptotic expansion in even Bernoulli numbers \f$B_2, \dots, B_{20}\f$ is evaluated in \f$z^{-2}\f$ by Horner's rule from highest to lowest index:
   \f[
   \psi(z) \sim \log z - \frac{1}{2z} - \sum_{k=1}^{10} \frac{B_{2k}}{2k\, z^{2k}}, \qquad
   \psi^{(n)}(z) \sim (-1)^{n+1} z^{-n} \left[ (n-1)! + \frac{n!}{2z} + \sum_{k=1}^{10} \frac{B_{2k} (2k+n-1)!}{(2k)!}\, z^{-2k} \right] \quad (n \ge 1).
   \f]

For \f$n \ge 1\f$, every term shares the sign of the result, so accumulation runs from the asymptotic part to \f$j = 0\f$ without cancellation. For \f$n = 0\f$, the recurrence sum and \f$\log z\f$ have opposite signs near \f$x_0\f$, so relative error diverges while absolute error stays at a few ulps. When \f$z > 10^{30}\f$, \f$z^{-n}\f$ can be subnormal. The bracket is therefore scaled by \f$2^{600}\f$ before multiplying powers of \f$z^{-1}\f$ and descaled by \f$2^{-600}\f$ afterward, so only the final product rounds as a subnormal.

## Accuracy

Accuracy is measured against 200-bit `mpmath` (2023) references over approximately 20000 positive arguments per function, with dense sampling near \f$x = 1.0\f$, \f$x = 2.0\f$, and \f$x_0\f$. See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for the definitions of \ref accuracy_ulp "ulp", \ref accuracy_ulp1 "ulp1", correctly rounded share, and \ref accuracy_bounds "test bounds".

| Function | Backend | Max ulp | Max ulp1 | Correctly rounded share | Test bound |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| \ref laplace::math::lgamma "lgamma()" | gcc | 2.42 | 2.42 | 0.72 | 4.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::lgamma "lgamma()" | MSVC host | 2.42 | — | — | 4.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::lgamma "lgamma()" | CUDA | 2.42 | 2.42 | 0.74 | 4.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::digamma "digamma()" | gcc | 2.14 | 2.14 | 0.82 | 3.5 ulp1 |
| \ref laplace::math::digamma "digamma()" | CUDA | 2.14 | 2.14 | 0.84 | 3.5 ulp1 |
| \ref laplace::math::trigamma "trigamma()" | gcc | 1.82 | 1.57 | 0.82 | 3.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::trigamma "trigamma()" | CUDA | 2.03 | 2.03 | 0.82 | 3.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::own::digamma "own::digamma()" | — | unbounded at root | 8.1 | — | — |
| \ref laplace::math::own::trigamma "own::trigamma()" | — | 2.89 | 2.11 | — | — |
| \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma(2, x)" | — | 3.36 | — | — | 5.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma(3, x)" | — | 5.26 | — | — | 8.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma(4, x)" | — | 6.34 | — | — | 9.5 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma(5, x)" | — | 7.90 | — | — | 12.0 ulp |
| \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma(6, x)" | — | 9.44 | — | — | 14.0 ulp |

For `own::polygamma()` at orders \f$n \in \{2, \dots, 6\}\f$, errors peak near \f$x = 1.0\f$, where up to 15 recurrence terms of \f$n + 1\f$ products are summed. Across the reference set, relative errors stay below \f$2.1 \times 10^{-15}\f$. For comparisons with platform libraries and host-only Boost polygamma, see `docs/numerics.md`.

## Speed

Evaluation times in nanoseconds per call were measured across \f$2^{23}\f$ arguments with exponents uniform in \f$[-5, 15]\f$. On one gcc thread, one MSVC host thread, 16 OpenMP threads, and CUDA, `lgamma()` takes 81.2, 21.9, 5.5, and 2.86 ns. On the same backends, `digamma()` takes 22.7, 10.7, 1.70, and 0.53 ns, and `trigamma()` takes 5.4, 6.7, 0.54, and 0.37 ns. Across orders \f$n = 2\f$ to \f$6\f$, `polygamma()` takes 15.8, 17.8, 21.0, 23.9, and 27.6 ns on one gcc thread, and 0.91, 2.40, 2.25, 1.75, and 1.57 ns on CUDA. For `own::digamma()` and `own::trigamma()`, times are 34.0 and 12.7 ns on one gcc thread, and 0.65 and 0.83 ns on CUDA.

## Example

The following example evaluates \f$\log \Gamma(x)\f$, \f$\psi(x)\f$, and \f$\psi^{(2)}(x)\f$ inside a `Kokkos::parallel_for` kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/math/special.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void compute_gamma_functions(const Kokkos::View<const double*>& x,
                             Kokkos::View<double*>& lg,
                             Kokkos::View<double*>& psi,
                             Kokkos::View<double*>& pg2,
                             int n) {
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_gamma", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    const double x_val = x(i);
    lg(i)  = laplace::math::lgamma(x_val);
    psi(i) = laplace::math::digamma(x_val);
    pg2(i) = laplace::math::polygamma(2, x_val);
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions

Functions in `<laplace/math/special.hpp>` are called in `laplace::math`, and Laplace's fallback routines in `<laplace/math/polygamma.hpp>` are defined in `laplace::math::own`.

- \ref laplace::math::lgamma "lgamma()" — \f$\log \Gamma(x)\f$ via Boost.Math
- \ref laplace::math::digamma "digamma()" — \f$\psi(x)\f$ via Boost.Math
- \ref laplace::math::trigamma "trigamma()" — \f$\psi'(x)\f$ via Boost.Math
- \ref laplace::math::polygamma "polygamma()" — \f$\psi^{(n)}(x)\f$ for \f$n \in \{0, \dots, 6\}\f$, dispatching to `digamma()`, `trigamma()`, or `own::polygamma()`
- \ref laplace::math::own::polygamma "own::polygamma()" — \f$\psi^{(n)}(x)\f$ for \f$n \in \{0, \dots, 6\}\f$ via recurrence and asymptotic expansion
- \ref laplace::math::own::digamma "own::digamma()" — \f$\psi(x)\f$ via `own::polygamma(0, x)`
- \ref laplace::math::own::trigamma "own::trigamma()" — \f$\psi'(x)\f$ via `own::polygamma(1, x)`

## References

- Boost.Math, C++ library of special functions and numerical tools, https://www.boost.org/libs/math
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §5.2 Definitions (Gamma Function), https://dlmf.nist.gov/5.2
*/
